**Proof.**

Let $S \in M_{n \times n}(\mathbb{C})$ be invertible, and let $x \in \mathfrak{gl}_S(n, \mathbb{C})$, so by definition
$$x^T S = -S x.$$

Since $S$ is invertible, we may right-multiply both sides by $S^{-1}$:
$$x^T S S^{-1} = -S x S^{-1},$$
which simplifies to
$$x^T = -S x S^{-1}.$$

Now take the trace of both sides. On the left, $\operatorname{tr}(x^T) = \operatorname{tr}(x)$, since the trace is invariant under transposition. On the right, using the cyclic property of the trace ($\operatorname{tr}(ABC) = \operatorname{tr}(CAB)$):
$$\operatorname{tr}(S x S^{-1}) = \operatorname{tr}(S^{-1} S x) = \operatorname{tr}(x).$$

Therefore
$$\operatorname{tr}(x) = \operatorname{tr}(x^T) = \operatorname{tr}(-S x S^{-1}) = -\operatorname{tr}(S x S^{-1}) = -\operatorname{tr}(x).$$

This gives $\operatorname{tr}(x) = -\operatorname{tr}(x)$, hence $2\operatorname{tr}(x) = 0$, and since we are working over $\mathbb{C}$ (characteristic zero),
$$\operatorname{tr}(x) = 0. \qquad \blacksquare$$

